  "chapter_notes": {
    "summary": "James 4 is saturated with Torah allusions. The most striking one-way marker is rachil (gossip/slander, v11) from Leviticus 19:16 (lo telekh rachil -- do not go about as a slanderer). Greek has katalalein (speak against), a generic word with no Torah connection. The plural setanim (adversaries, v7) again differs from Greek's singular diabolos (the devil). Torah (v5) is used directly for the authoritative source. The chapter uses nokem venoter (avenging and bearing grudges, v2) -- the exact two prohibitions from Leviticus 19:18. Masa umatan (buying and selling/commerce, v13) is a Hebrew idiom with no Greek equivalent. Ruach (breath/spirit/wind, v14) for human life echoes Genesis, where Greek has atmos (vapor/mist)."
  }
